{% set title = "Add a module" %}
{% include "ai/skills/_header.md.j2" %}

A module is one business area with its own tables, endpoints and rules
(`orders`, `invoices`). Name it by its plural, the way its URL reads.

**First, check it is a new module.** Data that always changes together with an
existing module (order lines with orders) is a new model *inside* that module:

```bash
moarch-py create model <module> <plural_name>  # e.g. moarch-py create model orders order_lines
```

adds its class, Create/Update/Read schemas and repository to that module's
files, registers it in `db/models.py` and writes a repository test. Then give
it its columns (a `ForeignKey("orders.id")` to its parent), use its repository
from the module's service, and create the migration. Stop here: the rest of
this skill is for a new module.

## 1. Scaffold

```bash
moarch-py create module <plural_name>         # e.g. moarch-py create module order_items
```

It writes `src/{{ name.package }}/modules/<name>/` (`public`, `models`, `schemas`,
`repository`, `service`, `router`, `dependencies`) and
`tests/modules/<name>/` (API and public tests), and registers the router in
`api/v1/router.py` and the model in `db/models.py` at the `# moarch:` anchors.
The scaffold is a working CRUD on a single `name` column; the next steps make
it the real thing. Run `uv run pytest` once now: the generated tests pass.

## 2. The model (`models.py`)

Replace `name` with the real columns. `Mapped[str]` is NOT NULL;
`Mapped[str | None]` is nullable. Give strings a length (`String(200)`), money
a `Numeric(12, 2)` (never `float`), times `DateTime(timezone=True)`. A link to
another module's table is a `ForeignKey("<table>.id")` column (a string, so no
import of that module) plus, when needed, a
`relationship()`. Add `index=True` to columns you filter or sort by, and
`unique=True` where the product says "only one".

## 3. The schemas (`schemas.py`)

- `<Name>Create`: what a client must send to create one, with `Field`
  constraints (`min_length`, `gt=0`, `EmailStr`…) so bad input is a 422 before
  any code runs.
- `<Name>Update`: the same fields, every one optional with `default=None`.
- `<Name>Read`: what the API returns. Never include secrets (a password hash)
  or internal fields.

## 4. The rules (`service.py`)

Put every business rule here: uniqueness (`ConflictError`), ownership
(`PermissionDeniedError`), state transitions ("a paid order cannot be
edited"). Each public method commits once at the end. A rule that needs data
from another module calls a function in that module's **`public.py`**, never
its service, repository or models. If the function is missing, add it there.

## 5. Queries (`repository.py`)

A query the service needs and `BaseRepository` lacks becomes a named method
(`get_by_email`, `list_unpaid_for_customer`) built with `select()`. Avoid N+1
queries: load relationships with `selectinload` in the query that needs them.

## 6. Endpoints (`router.py`)

Keep each endpoint to: parse → one service call → return a schema. Add the
endpoints the product needs and delete the ones it must not have (many
resources should never be deleted).

## 7. The public interface (`public.py`)

List only what other modules need today; start with the generated
`find_<name>` or delete it. Every function returns schemas (never a model),
takes the caller's session and never commits. List each name in `__all__`:
it is the interface at a glance, and mypy `--strict` refuses to let another
module import a schema `public.py` only imported unless it is listed there.
{% if options.migrations %}

## 8. Migration

```bash
uv run alembic revision --autogenerate -m "create <name>"
```

Read the generated file in `migrations/versions/`: check the columns,
nullability, indexes and the `downgrade()`. Then `uv run alembic upgrade head`.
{% endif %}

## {{ 9 if options.migrations else 8 }}. Tests

Update the generated API and public tests to the real fields, and add one
test per rule in the service (see `moarch-write-tests`). Then
`uv run lint-imports` must still pass.

